  "version": 1,
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Tank as One Whole: Pipe Rates and Partial Filling",
    "description": "Turn pipe times into rates and use them for whole or partial tanks.",
    "teachingApproach": "Begin with one tank as the number 1. A fill time becomes a fraction of the tank per hour; an emptying time becomes a negative fraction. Build from one pipe to partial tanks, remaining fractions, clock-time questions, and litre-per-minute equivalents. Each practice question starts by identifying how much of the tank must actually change before dividing by the rate.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 28,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 16, Pipes and Cisterns. Approved scope: Teach the one-tank model: if a pipe fills a tank in x hours, its rate is 1/x tank per hour; if an outlet empties in y hours, its rate is −1/y. Start with one pipe, then partial tanks and remaining fractions, and connect fraction-per-hour language to litres-per-minute language. Include questions where a tank is already partly full, where only part of a tank must be emptied, and where a start clock time is converted into a finish time. The learner should always identify the amount of tank still to be filled or emptied before dividing by the net rate."
  },
  "scriptId": "pc-01-tank-as-one-whole-pipe-rates",
  "entryNodeId": "start",
  "schemaVersion": 1
